## Finding

The export dated 4 October does not support the old claim of one issue with one queued fix.
The groups below are computed from distinct report IDs, tenant locale, booking form and
intake channel. Browser locale and intake tags are not authoritative. Counts describe this
export, not today's live queue, and are counts of reports, not unique people or bookings.

<IssueLinks />

## Separate failures

The shared parser reads dates month first even when the tenant writes day first. A date such
as 7 April can become 4 July. App and email reports, and booking-v2 form reports, fall into
this group under the exported classifier. The parser fix must respect the tenant's format;
changing the hint alone cannot fix these paths.

Booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` below its date field while its form reads dates in the
day-first tenant's format. People following that hint enter a date that is then interpreted
the other way around. Correct the hint to match the form's actual parsing, with locale-aware
examples and validation. These form reports need their own ticket and acceptance tests.

These are the causes assigned by the KB's executable model. We have not reproduced either
failure against a running product, and the export contains no product implementation.

## Evidence and exceptions

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The exported `CauseTable` still calls the smaller group “no ticket of its own”: that describes
the source desk's state. The tracking table above links the issues filed during this review.
Nothing here changes `mergedInto` in the historical source data.

The desk flagged R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344 and R-41620 as observations that read
as a one-day move. They remain in the parser group under the tenant-based model, not in an
invented timezone category. Their bodies include a June-only shift, a cross-city discrepancy
and an invite said to arrive before booking. Preserve these observations and obtain actual
timestamps, timezone settings, original inputs and stored values before claiming the parser
fix explains them. In particular, R-41620 shows that reports continued near the export date.

## What the queue shows

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports arrived from April onward, but merges began in July. A merge spike is desk activity,
not proof that the failures began that week. Reports continued after the July consolidation:
“known” did not mean resolved. The chart is bounded by the export; it cannot establish what
happened after 4 October or identify a fix deployment.

## Desk action

Use [the revised triage rule](/kb/triage/dedupe) and
[decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause). Read the body, look up the tenant,
and compare cause and entry path before merging. Unknown tenants and contradictory evidence
need investigation. Do not resolve either issue until its fix is tested; keep the unusual
observations visible during verification.

